app_dea : Diameter Edge Agent extension for freeDiameter
==========================================================

This extension turns a plain freeDiameter relay into a Diameter Edge Agent
(DEA): a node positioned at the boundary between a trusted (internal)
Diameter network and the outside world (roaming partners, interconnect,
other operators), enforcing privacy, security and routing policy on
messages crossing that boundary.

This is a multi-phase effort. This README tracks what is implemented and
what remains, so the extension can be extended without losing sight of the
full target scope.


Core mechanism (how it hooks into freeDiameter)
------------------------------------------------

freeDiameter routing pipeline, for a message that is not handled locally
(see include/freeDiameter/libfdcore.h, "ROUTING" section):

  received -> routing-in -> [fd_rt_fwd_register callbacks] -> routing-out -> sent

The daemon itself adds a Route-Record AVP for the sending peer BEFORE our
forwarding callback runs (p_psm.c), and routing-out uses Route-Record to
avoid sending a message back the way it came. This means our egress callback
sees the "real" Route-Record chain and can safely rewrite it before the
routing-out logic consumes it.

- `dea_fwd_req` is registered with `RT_FWD_REQ`: runs on every forwarded
  request. This is where masking happens.
- `dea_fwd_ans` is registered with `RT_FWD_ANS`: runs on the matching
  answer. The daemon associates every answer with its request via the
  Hop-by-Hop Id automatically, so `fd_hook_get_request_pmd()` reliably
  retrieves whatever we stashed on the request.
- Request/answer correlation uses `fd_hook_data_register` +
  `fd_hook_get_pmd` / `fd_hook_get_request_pmd` (libfdcore.h). This gives a
  scratch struct (`struct fd_hook_permsgdata`, defined in app_dea.h) that
  lives exactly as long as one transaction. It does NOT persist across a
  whole Diameter session (multiple transactions sharing a Session-Id) --
  see "Phase 2" below for what that requires instead.
- Session-scoped state (Phase 2, Session-Id pairing) uses freeDiameter's own
  session store instead: `fd_msg_sess_get()` resolves/creates a
  `struct session` from a message's Session-Id AVP, and
  `fd_sess_handler_create` + `fd_sess_state_store` / `fd_sess_state_retrieve`
  attach our own data to it (`struct sess_state`, defined in app_dea.h). This
  is a different mechanism from the per-transaction hook data above: it is
  keyed by Session-Id content (not by Hop-by-Hop Id) and survives for the
  configured `session_id_lifetime` of inactivity, i.e. across the whole
  Diameter session, not just one request/answer pair.


Phase 1 -- IMPLEMENTED
------------------------

Topology hiding for the internal -> external direction:

- Peer/realm classification via config (`internal_realm` directives).
  A request is masked only when `Origin-Realm` matches a configured
  internal realm AND `Destination-Realm` does not (fail-safe: anything
  ambiguous, e.g. a missing AVP, is left untouched rather than risk
  mangling legitimate internal traffic).
- `Origin-Host` masking: the real value is saved into the per-transaction
  data and replaced with the configured `hidden_identity` pseudonym.
- `Route-Record` masking: all instances are stripped and replaced with a
  single entry carrying `hidden_identity`, so the DEA itself remains
  attributable without exposing the internal hop chain.
- Config parser (`dea_conf.y` / `dea_conf.l`), following the same
  Flex/Bison pattern as `app_redirect` and `rt_rewrite`.

Deliberately NOT done in Phase 1 (tracked below): Origin-Realm is left
untouched (partners need it for routing/billing -- only node identity is
sensitive, not the operator realm), Session-Id is left untouched (see
Phase 2), CER/CEA capability exchange is left untouched, nothing is
enforced about which Application-Ids are allowed to cross the boundary.


Phase 2 -- Session-Id pseudonymization -- IMPLEMENTED (opt-in)
------------------------------------------------------------------

Session-Id AVPs commonly embed the creating node's Diameter Identity as
their first segment (`DiameterIdentity;hi32;lo32[;opt]`, RFC 6733 §8.8).
Left as-is, this leaks the real internal Origin-Host for the entire session
lifetime, defeating Phase 1's Origin-Host masking on every subsequent
message of the same session. `hide_session_id` (default OFF, see below)
closes this gap: on the first internal->external message of a session, the
Session-Id is replaced with a generated pseudonym, and every later message
of that session -- in both directions, requests and answers -- is
consistently rewritten between the real and masked value.

- **Why opt-in, not default-on like Phase 1**: RFC 6733 expects relay/proxy
  agents to leave Session-Id untouched -- it is meant to stay constant
  end-to-end for session correlation and CDR reconciliation between
  operators. Rewriting it is a deliberate, documented deviation (as used by
  real DEA deployments per GSMA IR.88) made only for topology-hiding
  privacy, not something to silently turn on for every operator.
- **Storage**: reuses freeDiameter's own session store (`fd_sess_*` in
  libfdproto.h) rather than a custom hash table. `fd_msg_sess_get()` on the
  live message resolves the real session; a masked pseudonym session is
  registered with `fd_sess_fromsid()`. Each side stores the *other* side's
  Session-Id string as its `struct sess_state` (`fd_sess_handler_create` /
  `fd_sess_state_store` / `fd_sess_state_retrieve`).
- **Why `fd_sess_fromsid()` and not `fd_sess_new()` to mint the masked
  session**: `fd_sess_new()`'s doc states the new session's initial message
  refcount is 1, released only once a live `struct msg` attaches to it via
  `fd_msg_sess_get()`. Our synthetic masked session has no guarantee of ever
  getting such an attachment on its own, which would pin it in memory
  forever (confirmed by reading `libfdproto/sessions.c` directly, not just
  the header doc). `fd_sess_fromsid()` does not touch that refcount, so we
  generate the RFC-shaped Session-Id string ourselves (mirroring
  `fd_sess_new()`'s own `DiamId;hi32;lo32` format, see `gen_masked_sid()` in
  `dea_hiding.c`) and register it with `fd_sess_fromsid()` instead.
- **Retrieve is destructive**: `fd_sess_state_retrieve()`'s doc says the
  state is detached from the session once retrieved -- a new
  `fd_sess_state_store()` is required to keep it attached. Every read in
  `dea_rewrite_session_id()` immediately stores it back. Without
  synchronization this is a race between two threads handling two messages
  of the same brand-new session concurrently (both could see "no pairing"
  and mint two different masked Session-Ids for the same real session), so
  the whole retrieve-then-restore-or-mint sequence runs under one global
  mutex (`dea_sid_lock`). This is a known Phase 4 scalability limit (it
  serializes Session-Id rewriting across *all* sessions, not just
  same-session concurrent access) -- acceptable for this foundation, flagged
  for revisiting if profiling ever shows contention.
- **Pairing expiry**: both sides of a pairing get their
  `fd_sess_settimeout()` refreshed to the *same* timestamp on every reuse
  (`session_id_lifetime` config, default 86400s of inactivity). Both
  therefore expire together in the same expiry-thread pass. We deliberately
  do NOT cross-destroy the paired session from within the cleanup callback
  (i.e. have session A's cleanup call `fd_sess_destroy()` on session B): the
  reentrancy of that call (B's own cleanup firing while A's is still
  running, immediately trying to destroy A again) could not be fully
  verified as safe by reading `libfdproto/sessions.c` alone, and the
  same-timestamp approach already achieves synchronized expiry without that
  risk.
- **NOT done in Phase 2**: reversible pseudonyms for subscriber identity
  (IMSI, User-Name, Subscription-Id -- see below), and no attempt to detect
  or reject a masked Session-Id being reused/replayed by a malicious peer
  (see Phase 3 fraud detection).


Phase 2.5 -- Subscriber identity privacy (not started)
------------------------------------------------------------

- **Reversible pseudonym scheme for HSS/MME/subscriber identity**: IMSI and
  other subscriber identifiers appearing in AVPs (e.g. User-Name,
  Subscription-Id) are not topology per se but are similarly sensitive
  across a roaming boundary in some deployments. Needs a policy decision
  (which AVPs, which direction) before implementation -- flagged here, not
  designed yet. Could reuse the same `fd_sess_*`-backed pairing mechanism
  built for Session-Id in Phase 2, or a dedicated store if the lifetime/scope
  needed differs (e.g. a pseudonym stable across sessions, not per-session).
- **IMSI/HSS/MME stateful mapping store leakage risks**: whatever store
  backs the above must itself not become a new leakage point (access
  control, at-rest protection, bounded TTL, no cross-tenant bleed in a
  multi-tenant deployment).


Phase 3 -- Interconnect policy & fraud detection
---------------------------------------------------

- **Application-Id allow-list for interconnect**: reject/drop requests
  whose `Application-Id` (message header, see `struct msg_hdr.msg_appl`)
  is not on a per-peer or per-realm allow-list. Natural fit for a second
  `fd_rt_fwd_register(..., RT_FWD_REQ, ...)` callback (or a branch inside
  `dea_fwd_req`), returning an error answer via
  `fd_msg_new_answer_from_req` + `fd_msg_rescode_set` + `fd_msg_send` (same
  idiom `app_redirect`/`rt_rewrite` use for in-place request -> answer
  conversion).
- **Fraud detection for spoofed roaming messages**: rule-based checks
  correlating claimed Origin-Realm/Origin-Host against the actual
  connected peer identity (`fd_msg_source_get` / `fd_peer_getbyid`) and
  against Route-Record consistency; rate-limiting/anomaly rules per peer.
  Needs a rule engine and a decision on false-positive handling
  (log-only vs. reject) before implementation.
- **CER/CEA / handshake fingerprint leaks**: freeDiameter's Capabilities
  Exchange (product name, firmware revision, supported
  Vendor-Id/Application-Id lists, and IP addresses in Host-IP-Address) is
  handled entirely in libfdcore, before any extension hook runs -- there is
  currently no extension hook point for CER/CEA content. Addressing this
  requires either a core-level change (out of scope for an extension) or
  running a separate stripped-down "mask" identity/instance at the network
  edge. Flagged as a known gap, not solvable purely as an app_dea feature.


Phase 4 -- Operability: HA, observability, performance
----------------------------------------------------------

- **High availability / failover**: freeDiameter's own peer/routing-out
  scoring (`fd_rt_out_register`, `FD_SCORE_*`) already provides multi-peer
  failover for the next hop; what's missing here is DEA-specific state
  replication -- Phase 2's Session-Id pairings live in each instance's own
  in-memory session store, so a failover mid-session currently loses the
  mapping (the session gets a fresh masked pseudonym on its next message,
  which is safe but changes the identity the external partner sees
  mid-session) across redundant DEA instances.
- **Metrics / privacy-aware observability**: counters and logs must not
  themselves leak what Phase 1/2 just hid (e.g. never log the real
  Origin-Host at a level that reaches external-facing dashboards). Natural
  fit for `fd_hook_register` on `HOOK_MESSAGE_ROUTING_FORWARD` /
  `HOOK_MESSAGE_SENT` / `HOOK_MESSAGE_RECEIVED` (read-only hooks, see
  `dbg_msg_timings` for the pattern) feeding metrics rather than rewriting.
- **Throttling**: per-peer / per-realm rate limiting, likely alongside the
  fraud-detection rule engine (Phase 3).
- **Testing**: fuzzing the config parser and the AVP-rewrite paths
  (malformed/oversized AVPs, repeated Route-Record floods, missing
  mandatory AVPs), plus dictionary coverage checks so masking rules don't
  silently no-op when a dictionary extension isn't loaded.
- **Performance targets**: not yet defined; needs a number (msgs/sec,
  p99 added latency) from whoever owns the deployment before it can drive
  design choices -- Phase 2's single global `dea_sid_lock` (see above) is
  the first thing to revisit if a target requires it.


Known limitations of the current code
----------------------------------------

- Origin-Host, Route-Record, and (opt-in) Session-Id are masked; anything
  else carrying topology information (Proxy-Info, vendor-specific AVPs with
  embedded hostnames) passes through unchanged -- see Phase 2.5/3.
- Masking runs on every internal->external request unconditionally once
  configured; there is no per-peer opt-out yet (e.g. a partner you
  deliberately don't hide topology from).
- All Session-Id rewriting is serialized behind one global mutex
  (`dea_sid_lock` in `dea_hiding.c`) -- correct but not scalable past
  whatever throughput that single critical section can sustain; see Phase 4.
- Session-Id pairings are process-local (no replication across redundant DEA
  instances) and are not persisted across a restart of the extension/daemon;
  see Phase 4 (HA).
- `dea_fwd_ans` handles Session-Id un/re-masking but otherwise only logs for
  Origin-Host/Route-Record; it is the intended integration point for
  Phase 3 features that need to act further on the answer path.
